To focus on an element found by `getElementsByTagName`, you can use the `focus()` method on the element. Here's an example:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName('input');
elements[0].focus(); // focus on the first input element
```
In this example, `getElementsByTagName` returns a `HTMLCollection` containing all the `<input>` elements on the page. We then access the first element in the collection using `[0]`, and call the `focus()` method on it to focus on that element.

If you want to focus on an element with a specific class or attribute, you can filter the collection using a regular expression or a loop:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName('input');
var focusedElement = null;
for (var i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
  if (elements[i].className === 'my-class') {
    focusedElement = elements[i];
    break;
  }
}
focusedElement.focus();
```
In this example, we loop through the collection and check if each element has a class of `my-class`. If it does, we assign it to the `focusedElement` variable and break out of the loop. Finally, we call `focus()` on the focused element.

Alternatively, you can use a querySelector and then call `focus()` on the result:
```
var focusedElement = document.querySelector('input.my-class');
focusedElement.focus();
```
This is a more concise and efficient way to focus on an element that matches a specific selector.

